268	 * Untracked files are compared through a scratch index (the base tree plus intent-to-add entries for every untracked
269	 * file, never the repository's own index), so git's rename detection pairs an unstaged move of a protected file with
270	 * its new name instead of showing that name as an unrelated new file. Rename and copy detection stop at git's
271	 * similarity threshold (and `--find-copies-harder` makes an untouched tracked protected file a copy source), so when
272	 * any protected path changed (moved, deleted, edited or added), every added file is omitted too and counted in
273	 * `omittedNew`: a protected file moved and rewritten, or one whose lines were copied out, may otherwise reach the diff
274	 * as an innocent new file. Independently of git's heuristics, an added file is omitted (and counted the same way) when
275	 * it equals a protected file present in the workspace, tracked, untracked or ignored, or shares a significant line
276	 * with one (see `protectedContent`); that covers `cp .env notes.txt` and a partial copy of any size.
277	 * Limits of that comparison, where credential redaction is the only guard: lines pasted from a protected file into a
278	 * file that already existed at the base (modified files are not line-compared); any re-formatting of a protected
279	 * file's lines (quoting, a changed separator, values only, re-encoding), because a line matches only exactly once
280	 * trimmed and is at least 8 characters; and a protected file whose lines are all shorter than that, which is caught
281	 * only by an exact whole-file match. The trade-off is over-omission: a new file sharing a common line (such as
282	 * NODE_ENV=development) with any protected file in the workspace, an ignored `.env*` included, is omitted and noted.
283	 * The pass listing ignored files takes up to IGNORED_LISTING_TIMEOUT_MS (about 6 s per 90k ignored files); a tree too
